KNOWNBUG
main.c
--string-abstraction
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
With --string-abstraction, the buffer size of a char array initialised with a
list ({'y', 'y', 'y', 'y'}, and also {'y', 'y', 'y', 0}) is unconstrained,
whereas it is set for an array initialised with a string literal or not
initialised at all. string_abstractiont::build_array only builds the abstract
string for an array object with an element that is equal to 0, but the
elements of the initialiser are typecasts of int constants (cast(0, char)),
so that the comparison `*it == 0' never holds and the object is left without
abstraction. Models that check the buffer size, such as memcpy
(__CPROVER_buffer_size(dst) >= n), therefore fail.
